<ModellingLayout title="Contexts and connections: agree enough, preserve what matters" description="Different professional communities can connect their models without pretending every shared word has one meaning. Give definitions an accountable home and make the agreements between them inspectable.">
  <section aria-labelledby="meaning">
    <h2 id="meaning"><span id="same-label" aria-hidden="true"></span>The same word can be doing different work</h2>
    <p>A surveyor asks which physical subject was inspected. A participant in a property transaction asks which evidence supports an assessment. Both may refer to “the property report”, while concentrating on different relationships and responsibilities. The disagreement is not necessarily an error to eliminate; it is something to understand.</p>
    <p>A <a href="/glossary#bounded-context">bounded context</a> gives a working language and model an accountable home. Within it, practitioners can resolve ambiguous definitions and consider changes coherently. Across contexts, explicit agreements explain where meanings coincide, where they differ and what information can travel between them.</p>
    <p>This is not permission for each community to invent its own truth. Evidence, identity criteria and the semantics of shared languages still apply. A building does not become a title record because one system uses “property” for both.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="boundary">
    <h2 id="boundary"><span id="discover-boundaries" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="contexts" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="homes" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="semantic-home" aria-hidden="true"></span>Use the documented boundaries to locate meaning</h2>
    <p>The context boundaries for this development phase are established inputs. Departments, working groups and applications can help us find people and evidence, but they do not replace those documented semantic homes. A single application may still serve several communities whose meanings need to remain distinct.</p>
    <p>Within an established context, ask who can explain a definition, which identity rules they use and who must review a change. Across documented contexts, write the differing definitions and consequences before deciding whether to reconcile the terms or keep a qualified connection.</p>
    <p>Within one context, do not use mappings to disguise contradictory definitions. Resolve the model’s own language. Between genuinely distinct contexts, do not force a central definition merely to avoid maintaining an explicit agreement.</p>
  </section>

<figure class="modelling-figure" data-modelling-figure="context-mapping-bridge">
    <ModellingVisual kind="context-mapping-bridge" />
    <dl>
      <dt>The strategic agreement</dt><dd>How two contexts collaborate, influence one another or deliberately remain separate.</dd>
      <dt>The term correspondence</dt><dd>How two particular defined concepts relate, with evidence and limits.</dd>
      <dt>The bridge</dt><dd>Which exact reviewed term mappings apply to that strategic relationship.</dd>
    </dl>
    <figcaption>A relationship between communities does not prove equivalence between their terms. This conceptual map is not a network or deployment diagram.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section aria-labelledby="harbour-court">
    <h2 id="harbour-court"><span id="property-pack" aria-hidden="true"></span>Connect the Harbour Court evidence without flattening it</h2>
    <p>In our fictional case, a surveying context supplies a description of the 12 August inspection. A receiving assessment context needs to know which report version supports the information it uses. The parties can agree how those references are exchanged without redefining an inspection as a report or treating the 20 August correction as another visit.</p>
    <p>The strategic agreement explains their relationship and responsibility for changes. A separate mapping describes a particular correspondence between concepts in their vocabularies. It may be exact, close, broader or another justified relation—or the reviewers may conclude that no mapping is appropriate.</p>
    <p>The useful outcome is not necessarily one merged term. It may be a clear translation that preserves the distinction the recipient needs. If the recipient cannot tell a corrected report from a new inspection, information has been lost even though the exchange succeeded technically.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="transfer">
    <h2 id="transfer">Try the same habit in another exchange</h2>
    <aside class="callout callout--note modelling-callout">
      <div class="callout__label">Fictional conveyancing transfer</div>
      <p>One sender uses “completion” for a transaction event; another label concerns completion of a system task. What definitions, subjects and practical consequences would establish whether those meanings correspond? Do not treat them as agreed industry definitions or force a <a href="/glossary#mapping">mapping</a> from the shared word alone.</p>
    </aside>
    <p>Use a compact review habit rather than a new form:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Subject:</strong> what is the statement about?</li>
      <li><strong>Context:</strong> which documented setting gives it meaning?</li>
      <li><strong>Statement:</strong> what exactly is being claimed?</li>
      <li><strong>Ordinary case:</strong> when does the wording work?</li>
      <li><strong>Counterexample:</strong> when could it mislead?</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence needed:</strong> what would support or change the interpretation?</li>
      <li><strong>Review question:</strong> what should the responsible people decide?</li>
    </ol>
    <p>More than one interpretation may be defensible, or the available evidence may be insufficient. This fictional prompt is not an agreed mapping or professional rule.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="common">
    <h2 id="common">Let common agreements earn their place</h2>
    <p>A small common agreement can be powerful when participants understand and need it. It still requires ownership, definitions, change review and evidence. “Small” is not an excuse for vagueness, and “common” does not mean every locally useful distinction must move into a central model.</p>
    <p>Start with the shared question and the minimum meaning required to answer it. Reuse an established definition when it fits. Qualify a mapping when differences matter. Introduce a local term when the evidence reveals a real gap. None of those choices is inherently a failure of interoperability.</p>
    <p>The OPDA method uses foundational analysis to challenge identity and dependence while keeping domain models out of an externally inherited upper-ontology hierarchy. Local ownership and shared discipline work together; they are not competing claims to absolute authority.</p>
  </section>

  <aside class="callout callout--note modelling-callout">
    <div class="callout__label">What does an implementer need to record?</div>
    <p>The selected method separates a scoped active context-map projection, its qualified strategic relationships and the individual mapping records applicable to each relationship. Upstream/downstream direction is different from a term mapping’s subject/object order. A named graph is not a bounded context, and a vocabulary identifier is not proof of its owner.</p>
    <p>The <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/context-map-records">context-map record chapter</a> explains active-map scope and its deliberate history boundary. <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/mapping-records">Mapping records</a> explains the source-version and justification contract.</p>
  </aside>

  <section aria-labelledby="source-contract">
    <h2 id="source-contract">What this explanation rests on</h2>
    <p>OPDA <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0038">ODR-0038</a>, <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0045">ODR-0045</a> and <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0060">ODR-0060</a> govern context ownership; <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0064">ODR-0064</a> distinguishes strategic relationships and exact mapping applicability; <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0063">ODR-0063</a> R1 supplies the external-upper-ontology inheritance boundary. Adoption provenance: upstream revision <code>67174057e6384b79d0b28b7736fe70a66e112895</code>, 5 September 2026. See <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/standards-and-decisions">standards and decisions</a>. The Harbour Court communities and their agreements are illustrative, not adopted OPDA boundaries.</p>
  </section>
</ModellingLayout>
